2026-09-27 — benchmark/standard-library import isolation

RED in a fresh macOS Python 3.9 process with tools/ladder on sys.path:
  import e0 removes the caller's ladder search path;
  import mixed fails with ModuleNotFoundError: subject;
  import shutil, gzip fails because the imported zlib benchmark has no
    Z_SYNC_FLUSH attribute.
The existing CLI --help control passes. Preloading e0 and reinserting the
directory merely hides the defect; it is not the repair.

Cause: importing shutil can import zlib. The benchmark file is named zlib.py
and used to execute its lane setup and permanently remove its directory even
when imported as the standard codec. sys.modules['zlib'] then held the wrong
module. Existing in-process unit tests had already imported stdlib consumers,
which masked this fresh-process ordering failure.

Repair: when resolved as module zlib, this entry point loads the actual codec
outside its own real directory and replaces its module slot without changing
the caller's search path. CLI execution and a distinctly named benchmark import
retain the existing Subject, version, options and normal entry point. Real-path
filtering also excludes a symlinked alias of the benchmark directory.

Six fresh-process controls PASS on macOS Python 3.9 and sealed Linux Python:
  e0 import/search path; mixed import; gzip and codec round trips;
  symlinked path; named Subject import; legacy zlib CLI --help.
The first Linux test attempt incorrectly required zlib.__file__; Linux's built-in
codec has none. The final assertion checks module-spec origin plus actual byte
round trips, admitting both built-in and extension codecs, not weaker behavior.

All 290 ladder Python tests PASS, targeted Ruff and diff checks PASS. The final
origin/gzip assertion adjustment was rerun in both six-case platform groups.
No application receipt, source, compiler binary, test expectation or pin changes.
This is harness closure, not a new zlib/application campaign PASS.

Commands:
  python3 -m unittest discover -s tools/ladder/tests -p test_import_isolation.py
  python3 -m unittest discover -s tools/ladder/tests
  container run --rm --platform linux/arm64 --network none \
    -v /Users/onehrxn/dev/30days/c30:/work:ro -w /work evo-flight-lab:sealed \
    python3 -m unittest discover -s tools/ladder/tests -p test_import_isolation.py
The native container uses a read-only mount, not another checkout or disk image.
